<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=tank">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as landship</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tank is an armoured fighting vehicle combining heavy firepower, strong armour and the mobility of tracks and a powerful engine; the first British prototype, Little Willie, was built at William Foster &amp; Co. in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln, England</a>, in 1915. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> developed tanks separately and at the same time to break the deadlock of trench warfare in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; no single inventor can be named, though Walter Gordon Wilson and William Tritton played leading roles. The British Mark I first went into battle at the Somme in September 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tank.jpg" alt="Tank" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_I_series_tank.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tank is the 20th-century realisation of an ancient concept, that of giving troops mobile protection and firepower. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mobei">Battle of Mobei</a> in 119 BCE the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> used armoured heavy wagons, drawn up in rings, to shelter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, crossbowmen and infantry from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> cavalry. In the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-zizka">Jan Žižka</a> built armoured wagons carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, used effectively in several battles of the Hussite wars. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a>, armour plate and the continuous track were the key innovations leading to the modern tank.</p><p>The word tank was first applied in a military context in 1915 to keep the nature of the British landships secret. According to Albert Gerald Stern, secretary of the Landship Committee, at a conference on 24 December 1915 Thomas J. Macnamara suggested changing the committee's title for secrecy, and Mr d'Eyncourt proposed water carriers. Stern, who thought the initials of that title unsuitable, changed the word to tank, and the committee became the Tank Supply Committee. In France Colonel J. B. E. Estienne had the word char d'assaut preferred.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A tank is an armoured fighting vehicle meant as a primary offensive weapon in front-line ground combat. Its capability rests on firepower, protection and mobility, and designs differ in how they blend them. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the first designs, such as the British Mark I, were long and large in order to cross wide trenches and were classed as heavy tanks, while smaller vehicles such as the French Renault FT were classed as light tanks or tankettes. Improved suspension in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, such as the Christie system and the torsion-bar suspension of Ferdinand Porsche, greatly improved cross-country performance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was the first to begin moving from petrol to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diesel-engine">diesel engines</a>, with the Type 89B in 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The British and French built thousands of tanks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, unconvinced of their potential and short of resources, built only twenty. Commanding tanks in the field was difficult. Armoured bulkheads, engine noise, dust and smoke hindered communication, and radios were not portable or robust enough to be mounted in a tank, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morse-code">Morse code</a> transmitters were installed in some Mark IVs at Cambrai as messaging vehicles. Crews sometimes sent reports back by releasing carrier pigeons, and vehicles signalled to one another by hand or by semaphore flags, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and Soviet Army kept in use through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tank” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1915</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Lincoln, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Walter Gordon Wilson · William Tritton</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>France (1915)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>No single inventor can be named, and earlier designs, from Leonardo da Vinci’s to Lancelot de Mole’s of 1911, were never built.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine<span>1794</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Machine gun<span>1884</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=tank">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
